# HyperMarkDown as an extension

HyperMarkDown is one source HQL can read, integrated through an
[[hql/extensions|extension]] like any other. It is not the language's subject
and not a privileged case. [[hql]] is a general typed functional language, and
nothing in [[hql/core]] names HMD: the core has typed values, bindings,
functions and composition, and every way of reaching outside the program is an
extension.

Integration is planned, not implemented. There is no extension runtime, no
loader and no HMD dependency in the bootstrap.

## HQL is not an HMD query language

An extension connects the core to a capability it does not have. Reading a tree
of documents off a filesystem is one such capability; so is talking to Postgres,
to a graph store, or to bulk storage such as HDFS or an object bucket. These are
candidate sources, not a bundled list, and they are peers — the same language
computes over all of them, and a program that never touches a document is an
ordinary HQL program.

This has a practical consequence for the type vocabulary. Document types belong
to whichever extension supplies documents; they are not core types that other
sources have to be translated into. A relational source yields rows, not
documents pretending to be rows.

## Knowledge depends on the graph capability, not on HMD

Layering runs core → graph → knowledge, and HMD sits to one side of it.

- The **graph** capability is core: nodes, edges and traversal, including the
  two link directions [[doc-type|documents]] expose as `uplinks` and
  `downlinks`.
- The **knowledge** extension builds on that capability — concepts, relations,
  propositions and evidence are graph-shaped, so [[hql/knowledge]] requires
  graph and not the reverse. Plus `cards` becomes a mechanism that connected documents to knowledge graph.
- The **HMD** extension supplies documents. It does not supply knowledge.
  Interpreting documents as knowledge is the knowledge extension's work, and
  the interpretation is explicit, never a parse result.

So a vault of `.hmd` files is not knowledge because it is HMD. It becomes
knowledge when something reads it as such, and the same reading could run over
documents fetched from a database.

## HMD is a format, not a category of thing

An `.hmd` file is a document. An `.md` file is a document. Both project to
`Doc`, and the extension that reads a directory reads a collection of documents
without sorting them into two kinds. The relation between the formats is
inclusion — HyperMarkDown is an unchanged CommonMark base plus a hyper layer —
so `doc.format` says which body features are available to ask about, and
nothing more. Asking a Markdown document for its wikilinks is a well-formed
question with an empty answer.

What the format does **not** do is decide what a document *is*. There is no
divide under which `.hmd` files are cards and `.md` files are mere documents.
That distinction was a mistake about where card-ness comes from.

## A card is a physical representation of knowledge

`Card` is an additional data type, and its subject is knowledge rather than
storage. A card is the physical representation a piece of knowledge is given:
one document that stands for a concept, a relation or a claim, and that can
reference the concepts it explains.

That makes card-ness a knowledge notion carried by a document, not a property a
file acquires from its extension. A plain Markdown file can represent a concept.
An `.hmd` file can be a document that represents nothing in particular. The
reference goes from the card to the concept — a card explains concepts, and a
concept may be explained by more than one card, or by none.

[[card-type]] narrows `Card : Doc` on one condition: that the document sits in
a vault. Format is not among the conditions, because format does not make a
card. The vault is, because naming and `downlinks` need a namespace to be
answerable in — whether that requirement belongs to the language or only to a
host is open.

## What the HMD extension provides

The extension's job is to turn a workspace on disk into typed documents, and the
parsing work already exists. The inspected HyperMarkDown checkout was revision
`74487940adb82654207cfec4cfd934d03eeee8f9` of
[ewiger/hypermarkdown](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown). Paths below
refer to that revision:

- [`tools/hmd/src/hypermarkdown/parse.py`](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/blob/74487940adb82654207cfec4cfd934d03eeee8f9/tools/hmd/src/hypermarkdown/parse.py):
  `parse(path, text, rel)` returns a `Document` with parse diagnostics.
- [`model.py`](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/blob/74487940adb82654207cfec4cfd934d03eeee8f9/tools/hmd/src/hypermarkdown/model.py):
  `Document` retains text, the authored header block, `CardConfig`, headings, anchors, and
  links. Link records include target, fragment, display, embed status, and span.
- [`frontmatter.py`](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/blob/74487940adb82654207cfec4cfd934d03eeee8f9/tools/hmd/src/hypermarkdown/frontmatter.py):
  the reserved `tags`, `use`, `import`, and `nav` keys are checked; other keys
  remain user-owned. This is a partial schema, not a complete record type.
- [`resolve.py`](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/blob/74487940adb82654207cfec4cfd934d03eeee8f9/tools/hmd/src/hypermarkdown/resolve.py):
  `Workspace` owns documents and contextual resolution. Resolution distinguishes
  resolved, unresolved, ambiguous, and escaping references; precedence includes
  named imports, spine, imported search paths, and autodiscovery.

The repository README identifies the Python tool as canonical and the
TypeScript core as a second implementation sharing a conformance corpus. There
is no reason to write another HMD parser in this Rust bootstrap.

## Unresolved transport

The extension can call the canonical Python library to parse and resolve a
workspace, then expose a versioned projection to Rust through serialization or
an in-process bridge. That protocol is work to design, not an existing API we
assume is available. The projection translates each `Document` into a
language-facing document value, preserving provenance, document structure,
metadata, namespace context, relation outcomes, and diagnostics.

Reserved header keys can supply known field types; user fields need an explicit
schema or a deliberate unknown-value policy, and [[type-system]]
describes the progressive route rather than inferring a complete schema from one
example. Preserve ambiguity and missing references instead of choosing a
document silently — HMD treats an unresolved link as a warning on purpose.
Verify span coordinate conventions with Unicode fixtures before translating
Python positions into HQL UTF-8 byte diagnostics.

Keep HMD import and resolution rules inside the extension; `.hql` module
semantics are a separate design, recorded in [[hql/extensions]]. The extension
should use the HMD conformance corpus plus projection tests. The present core
has no HMD dependency and no document placeholder.

See [architecture](../models/domain/architecture.md), [[hypermarkdown]] for the
format, [[doc-type]] for the document type, [[card-type]] for the card type and
[[design-direction]] for what is still open.
